1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,848
A lamp hangs still.

2
00:00:01,848 --> 00:00:03,905
A tug of war rope stops dead.

3
00:00:03,905 --> 00:00:05,662
A bridge holds up a bus.

4
00:00:06,062 --> 00:00:08,170
Big forces act on all three.

5
00:00:08,170 --> 00:00:10,057
So why does nothing move?

6
00:00:10,507 --> 00:00:14,837
Aarav ties a rope across his study
window, and hangs his two kilogram bag

7
00:00:14,837 --> 00:00:15,892
in the middle.

8
00:00:16,242 --> 00:00:17,304
The rope sags.

9
00:00:17,304 --> 00:00:19,521
He pulls it tighter, and tighter.

10
00:00:19,521 --> 00:00:20,583
Still it sags.

11
00:00:20,583 --> 00:00:22,518
Why can it never go straight?

12
00:00:23,018 --> 00:00:25,012
The answer is equilibrium.

13
00:00:25,612 --> 00:00:29,266
A body is in equilibrium when
the net force on it is zero.

14
00:00:29,266 --> 00:00:32,456
All its forces, with their
directions, cancel out.

15
00:00:32,805 --> 00:00:34,824
Then there is no acceleration.

16
00:00:34,824 --> 00:00:38,122
It stays at rest, or moves
at a steady velocity.

17
00:00:38,522 --> 00:00:41,004
The simplest case: a lamp on one cord.

18
00:00:41,404 --> 00:00:42,898
Its weight pulls down.

19
00:00:42,898 --> 00:00:44,204
The cord pulls up.

20
00:00:44,204 --> 00:00:46,036
That pull is called tension.

21
00:00:46,436 --> 00:00:49,374
They must be equal,
opposite, and on one line.

22
00:00:49,374 --> 00:00:51,392
So the tension equals the weight.

23
00:00:52,042 --> 00:00:53,289
Now Aarav's bag.

24
00:00:53,289 --> 00:00:57,148
Two strings, each at an angle
theta to the horizontal.

25
00:00:57,498 --> 00:01:01,750
At sixty degrees, each string pulls
with eleven point three newtons.

26
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:03,978
The bag weighs nineteen point six.

27
00:01:04,378 --> 00:01:05,660
Pull them flatter.

28
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:09,122
At thirty degrees, each
tension is nineteen point six.

29
00:01:09,122 --> 00:01:10,356
The whole weight.

30
00:01:10,756 --> 00:01:13,013
At ten degrees, fifty six newtons.

31
00:01:13,013 --> 00:01:15,467
At five, over one hundred and twelve.

32
00:01:15,467 --> 00:01:17,874
On the graph, the tension shoots up.

33
00:01:18,274 --> 00:01:19,422
Slow-mo replay.

34
00:01:19,422 --> 00:01:22,782
Freeze, and split each
tension into two parts.

35
00:01:23,132 --> 00:01:26,413
The sideways parts are big,
but equal and opposite.

36
00:01:26,413 --> 00:01:27,339
They cancel.

37
00:01:27,739 --> 00:01:31,932
The upward parts are tiny, yet together
they must hold the whole weight.

38
00:01:32,582 --> 00:01:34,709
Now the rule, one step at a time.

39
00:01:35,059 --> 00:01:35,997
Step one.

40
00:01:35,997 --> 00:01:38,810
Equilibrium: the net force is zero.

41
00:01:38,810 --> 00:01:40,513
Sigma F equals zero.

42
00:01:41,263 --> 00:01:42,218
Step two.

43
00:01:42,218 --> 00:01:47,217
In each direction: sigma F x
is zero, and sigma F y is zero.

44
00:01:47,967 --> 00:01:49,024
Step three.

45
00:01:49,024 --> 00:01:52,930
Sideways: T one cos theta
equals T two cos theta.

46
00:01:52,930 --> 00:01:55,112
So both tensions are equal.

47
00:01:55,112 --> 00:01:56,296
Call them T.

48
00:01:57,046 --> 00:01:58,004
Step four.

49
00:01:58,004 --> 00:02:01,629
Upward: two T sine theta
equals the weight, W.

50
00:02:02,379 --> 00:02:03,473
Step five.

51
00:02:03,473 --> 00:02:06,526
So T equals W over two sine theta.

52
00:02:07,276 --> 00:02:08,369
Remember this.

53
00:02:08,369 --> 00:02:12,518
In equilibrium, the forces in
every direction add up to zero.

54
00:02:13,218 --> 00:02:15,154
Now, back to Aarav's rope.

55
00:02:15,154 --> 00:02:16,255
Link by link.

56
00:02:16,605 --> 00:02:20,880
Flatter strings mean a smaller angle
theta, so a smaller sine theta.

57
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,844
So each string gives
a smaller upward part.

58
00:02:23,844 --> 00:02:26,452
To hold the weight, the tension must grow.

59
00:02:26,852 --> 00:02:29,347
At zero degrees, sine theta is zero.

60
00:02:29,347 --> 00:02:32,095
No upward part, so no tension is enough.

61
00:02:32,095 --> 00:02:34,735
The rope can never go perfectly straight.

62
00:02:35,385 --> 00:02:36,479
Side by side.

63
00:02:36,479 --> 00:02:38,139
Two forces: the lamp.

64
00:02:38,139 --> 00:02:40,366
Equal, opposite, on one line.

65
00:02:40,766 --> 00:02:45,499
Three forces: the bag at thirty
degrees, all nineteen point six newtons.

66
00:02:45,499 --> 00:02:48,307
Tip to tail, they close into a triangle.

67
00:02:48,707 --> 00:02:51,803
The triangle closes, so
the net force is zero.

68
00:02:52,403 --> 00:02:53,477
Now you try.

69
00:02:53,477 --> 00:02:58,304
Can forces of three, four and nine
newtons keep a ring in equilibrium?

70
00:02:58,304 --> 00:02:59,639
Pause and decide.

71
00:03:02,839 --> 00:03:03,739
No!

72
00:03:03,307 --> 00:03:05,457
Three plus four is only seven.

73
00:03:05,457 --> 00:03:09,176
It can never reach nine, so
the triangle cannot close.

74
00:03:09,826 --> 00:03:10,877
Another pair.

75
00:03:10,877 --> 00:03:13,370
A raindrop falls at a steady speed.

76
00:03:13,370 --> 00:03:15,163
Drag balances its weight.

77
00:03:15,163 --> 00:03:17,759
It moves, yet it is in equilibrium.

78
00:03:18,159 --> 00:03:21,404
A ball thrown up stops
for an instant at the top.

79
00:03:21,404 --> 00:03:23,721
Its weight still pulls, unbalanced.

80
00:03:23,721 --> 00:03:25,952
At rest, yet not in equilibrium.

81
00:03:26,552 --> 00:03:27,482
Your turn.

82
00:03:27,482 --> 00:03:31,716
A parachutist drifts down at a
steady five metres per second.

83
00:03:31,716 --> 00:03:33,416
Is she in equilibrium?

84
00:03:33,416 --> 00:03:34,785
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:37,985 --> 00:03:38,885
Yes!

86
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:40,975
Steady velocity, zero acceleration.

87
00:03:40,975 --> 00:03:44,153
The drag on her parachute
balances her weight.

88
00:03:44,803 --> 00:03:46,539
Now, one worked example.

89
00:03:46,889 --> 00:03:51,472
A five kilogram lamp hangs from two
wires, at thirty and sixty degrees

90
00:03:51,472 --> 00:03:52,693
to the ceiling.

91
00:03:52,693 --> 00:03:54,121
Find both tensions.

92
00:03:54,621 --> 00:03:57,322
The weight is five times nine point eight.

93
00:03:57,322 --> 00:03:58,639
Forty nine newtons.

94
00:03:59,389 --> 00:04:03,190
Sideways: T one cos thirty
equals T two cos sixty.

95
00:04:03,190 --> 00:04:05,989
So T two is root three times T one.

96
00:04:06,739 --> 00:04:11,779
Upward: T one sine thirty, plus T
two sine sixty, equals forty nine.

97
00:04:11,779 --> 00:04:14,145
So two T one equals forty nine.

98
00:04:14,895 --> 00:04:19,712
T one is twenty four point five
newtons, and T two about forty two point

99
00:04:19,712 --> 00:04:20,612
four.

100
00:04:20,292 --> 00:04:22,416
The steeper wire carries more.

101
00:04:23,066 --> 00:04:24,532
Now, the exam trap.

102
00:04:24,882 --> 00:04:28,313
Many students say each
string holds half the weight.

103
00:04:28,313 --> 00:04:29,305
W over two.

104
00:04:29,705 --> 00:04:32,748
But only the upward
parts share the weight.

105
00:04:32,748 --> 00:04:34,882
T is W over two sine theta.

106
00:04:34,882 --> 00:04:38,739
W over two holds only for
strings hanging straight down.

107
00:04:39,389 --> 00:04:40,755
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:40,755 --> 00:04:44,103
In strings at angles, and
in which forces can balance.

109
00:04:44,703 --> 00:04:45,864
Exam level now.

110
00:04:45,864 --> 00:04:50,883
A two metre string joins two hooks one
point two metres apart, at one height.

111
00:04:50,883 --> 00:04:53,570
A four kilogram bag hangs at its middle.

112
00:04:53,570 --> 00:04:54,470
g is ten.

113
00:04:54,436 --> 00:04:55,696
Find the tension.

114
00:04:55,696 --> 00:04:56,906
Pause and solve.

115
00:05:02,306 --> 00:05:03,720
The answer is B.

116
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:05,287
Twenty five newtons.

117
00:05:05,787 --> 00:05:10,182
Each half is one metre, spanning
zero point six metres sideways.

118
00:05:10,182 --> 00:05:14,858
So cos theta is zero point six,
and sine theta, zero point eight.

119
00:05:15,358 --> 00:05:17,409
The weight is forty newtons.

120
00:05:17,409 --> 00:05:19,629
Two T sine theta equals forty.

121
00:05:20,129 --> 00:05:22,690
So T is forty over one point six.

122
00:05:22,690 --> 00:05:24,229
Twenty five newtons.

123
00:05:24,829 --> 00:05:26,291
Here is your recap.

124
00:05:26,291 --> 00:05:27,794
Screenshot this card.

125
00:05:30,994 --> 00:05:32,925
Can you answer these without help?

126
00:05:33,325 --> 00:05:34,225
One.

127
00:05:33,826 --> 00:05:37,201
Why can Aarav never pull
his rope perfectly straight?

128
00:05:38,101 --> 00:05:39,001
Two.

129
00:05:38,644 --> 00:05:42,202
Can forces of two, three
and six newtons balance?

130
00:05:42,202 --> 00:05:43,102
Why?

131
00:05:43,645 --> 00:05:44,545
Three.

132
00:05:44,287 --> 00:05:47,824
Is a ball at the top of
its throw in equilibrium?

133
00:05:47,824 --> 00:05:48,724
Why?

134
00:05:49,461 --> 00:05:53,095
Next time: a magnet pulls
a pin without touching it.

135
00:05:53,095 --> 00:05:55,521
How can a force cross empty space?

136
00:05:55,521 --> 00:05:57,559
Contact and non-contact forces.
